        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 610,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 807,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 611,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122972,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 611,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leydig cell tumour; granulosa cell tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 808,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 612,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122973,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 612,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours arise in the adrenal cortex.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 809,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 613,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122974,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 613,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with adrenal granulosa cell tumour present with irregular uterine bleeding and/or an abdominal mass {4687888; 19423096}. In contrast, patients with adrenal Leydig cell tumour typically present with virilization and have elevated serum testosterone and DHEAS, with pituitary suppression of LH.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 810,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 614,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122975,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 614,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Few cases have been reported {4687888; 19423096; 26126046; 6270941; 3000178; 3022613}; all occurred in postmenopausal women (median age: 60&nbsp;years).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 811,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 615,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122976,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 615,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 812,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 616,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122977,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 616,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histogenesis is uncertain {4687888; 19423096; 26126046; 6270941; 3000178; 3022613}. An origin from ectopic gonadal stroma is suggested by the close embryological origin of gonads and adrenals {9100075; 10385563; 19266086} and from reports of non-neoplastic Leydig cells in the adrenal cortex and within adrenal ganglioneuromas {4347248; 7190543; 6685441; 13749451}. Alternatively, they may arise from pluripotential mesenchymal cells exposed to high levels of gonadotropins or adrenocorticotropin {7190543}. Primitive spindle cells with Reinke-like crystals have been described in the adrenal cortex, supporting this hypothesis {4347248}. So-called ovarian thecal metaplasia in the adrenal supports this mechanism {3653882; 5110570; 851092; 21858518}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 819,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 623,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122978,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 623,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These solitary unilateral solid tumours are well circumscribed, often encapsulated, and only occasionally cystic. Tumour sizes range from 19&nbsp;to 90&nbsp;mm. The cut surface is yellow, white, or brown {6685441}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 813,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 617,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122979,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 617,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Like their ovarian adult granulosa cell counterparts, granulosa cell tumours display a microfollicular architecture with Call&ndash;Exner bodies and with macrofollicular, solid, and sarcomatoid patterns {29132723; 4687888; 19423096; 26126046}. Tumour cells are round to oval with scant cytoplasm and pale nuclei. Nuclear grooves are variably seen.</p>\r\n<p>Leydig cell tumour is composed of closely packed cell nests with a prominent intervening capillary network. Trabeculae or cords interrupted by oedema cause small cysts or pseudoglands. Tumour cells have abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and round to oval nuclei with small to moderate-sized nucleoli. Brown cytoplasmic lipochrome pigment is common. Reinke crystals are necessary for classification as Leydig cell tumour {6270941; 3000178; 3022613}.</p>\r\n<p>Sex cord tumours express SF1, &alpha;-inhibin, calretinin, and melan-A, but not adrenocortical-specific steroidogenic enzymes (CYP11B1, CYP11B2) {23240671; 19033865}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 814,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 618,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122980,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 618,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 815,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 619,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122981,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 619,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 818,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 622,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122982,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 97,
        "headingId": 622,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n\r\nFor granulosa cell tumour: a neoplasm composed of microfollicular architecture, large neoplastic cells, Call&ndash;Exner bodies, and round nuclei with irregular nuclear membranes and grooves\r\nLeydig cell tumour: a neoplasm composed of bland steroid-producing cells in closely packed nests, showing cytoplasmic Reinke crystals\r\n\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for inhibin, calretinin, SF1, and melan-A (MART1), and negativity for EMA and other keratins, in selected cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:45:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 816,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
